            "id": "pc2_s2_c1",
            "chapterNum": 1,
            "title": "International Standards- INTOSAI Principles and selected standards: ISSAI- 1, 10, 100, 200, 300, 400",
            "content": "### INTOSAI Pronouncements and Core ISSAI Standards\n\n1. **ISSAI 1: The Lima Declaration (1977)**:\n   - The Magna Carta of government auditing.\n   - Mandates that Supreme Audit Institutions (SAIs) must have constitutional and legal independence from the executive.\n   - Independence extends to financial autonomy, unrestricted access to records, and discretion in reporting.\n2. **ISSAI 10: Mexico Declaration on SAI Independence (2007)**:\n   - Identifies 8 core pillars of SAI independence (Constitutional framework, broad mandate, security of tenure, financial autonomy, freedom to report, unrestricted access, adequate powers, and discretion).\n3. **ISSAI 100: Fundamental Principles of Public Sector Auditing**:\n   - Three parties: Auditor, Responsible Party (Auditee Executive), and Intended Users (Legislature and Citizens).\n   - Subject matter and criteria (rules, benchmarks, performance targets).\n   - Audit types: Financial, Performance, Compliance.\n   - Professional skepticism, professional judgment, audit risk, materiality, and documentation.\n4. **ISSAI 200 (Financial Audit Principles)**: Reasonable assurance on true and fair presentation of accounts.\n5. **ISSAI 300 (Performance Audit Principles)**: Evaluating 3Es (Economy, Efficiency, Effectiveness) against sound criteria.\n6. **ISSAI 400 (Compliance Audit Principles)**: Assessing adherence to laws, regulations, and propriety.",
