            "content": "### Correlation Analysis in Audit Analytics\n\n1. **Concept of Correlation**: Measuring the strength and direction of linear association between two audit variables ($x$ and $y$).\n2. **Karl Pearson's Coefficient of Correlation ($r$)**:\n   - Ranges from $$-1$$ to $$+1$$.\n   - $$r = +1$$: Perfect positive correlation (e.g. increase in production volume perfectly matches raw material consumption).\n   - $$r = 0$$: No linear correlation.\n   - $$r = -1$$: Perfect negative correlation.\n3. **Audit Application**:\n   - Correlating bitumen purchase with road mileage constructed (detects diversion of materials).\n   - Correlating patient bed-occupancy with medical drug consumption (detects fake billing).\n   - Non-linear relationships and spurious correlations.",
            "gist": "Correlation measures association between variables (-1 to +1). Auditors use correlation to test consistency between inputs (funds/materials) and outputs (mileage/services).",
            "crux": "1. Value ranges between -1 and +1.\n2. Weak correlation between input consumption and output indicates potential fraud/waste.\n3. Correlation does not prove causation.",
