            "title": "Sampling techniques applied in typical audit situations (Random, Systematic, Stratified, MUS)",
            "content": "### Statistical Audit Sampling Methods\n\n1. **Simple Random Sampling**: Every voucher in the population has an equal chance of selection (using random number tables or software).\n2. **Systematic Sampling**: Selecting every $k$-th transaction ($k = N/n$) after a random start. *Risk*: Cyclical patterns in accounts.\n3. **Stratified Sampling**: Dividing population into homogeneous sub-populations (strata) based on value or risk (e.g. Stratum 1: > Rs 1 Crore (100% check); Stratum 2: Rs 10L to 1 Crore; Stratum 3: < Rs 10L).\n4. **Monetary Unit Sampling (MUS)**:\n   - Attribute and variable sampling method where each rupee acts as a sampling unit.\n   - High-value transactions automatically have higher probability of selection; ideal for financial audit of accounts.\n5. **Audit Sampling Risk**:\n   - *Risk of incorrect acceptance* (Beta risk / Type II error): Concluding no material misstatement exists when it does (affects audit effectiveness).\n   - *Risk of incorrect rejection* (Alpha risk / Type I error): Concluding misstatement exists when it does not (affects audit efficiency).",
            "gist": "Sampling methods: Simple Random, Systematic, Stratified (homogenous sub-groups), and Monetary Unit Sampling (MUS). Alpha risk affects efficiency; Beta risk affects effectiveness.",
            "crux": "1. Stratified sampling is standard for government expenditure (100% check of top stratum).\n2. MUS gives high-value items proportionate chance of selection.\n3. Beta risk (failing to detect misstatement) is the most dangerous audit risk.",
