            "title": "1.5 AI in Auditing: Leverage for data analysis, public LLM precautions, Responsible AI, risks in government AI systems",
            "content": "### Responsible AI, Data Privacy and Public LLM Risks in Audit\n\nnal power, and the availability of vast data. It has evolved from a theore cal concept to a transforma ve technology with far-reaching social acceptance and economic implica ons across diverse domains, in the last decade. Figure 1: Milestones in AI Development As AI con nues to evolve, its poten al to transform industries and drive innova on has assumed immense propor ons. For instance, in the present-day healthcare sector, AI algorithms are analysing medical images for early disease detec on, accelera ng drug discovery etc. The ﬁnancial sector leverages AI for fraud detec on, risk assessment, modelling etc. AI now powers virtual assistants and chatbots, recommenda on systems, and self-driving cars. AI systems have over the years rapidly converged and in some cases, even surpassed the human performance and capabili es in image recogni on, reading comprehension, language understanding etc. (Refer Image below) Figure 2: AI Development h ps://www.isaca.org/resources/glossary Artiﬁcial Intelligence Strategy Framework However, at the same me, like with any emerging technology, the adop on of AI has also raised important social and ethical considera ons, such as data privacy, algorithmic bias, and job displacement, etc. among others. 1.1. Ar ﬁcial Intelligence - Concepts AI technology involves various components, which start with a corpus of a large amount of data, which can be in diﬀerent forms such as text, images, etc. This data is then fed into machine learning models, which are algorithms designed to learn pa erns and rela onships. The popular models are Neural Networks, Decision trees, and Support vector machines etc. They are trained using learning algorithms, such as Supervised learning, Unsupervised learning, or Reinforcement learning (explained in 1.1 below). This enables the models to improve their performance on a given task over me. This requires signiﬁcant compu ng power, usually in the form of powerful processors (like Graphics Processing Units or GPUs), which is required to train and run these models eﬃciently. So ware libraries and frameworks provide pre-built tools and components for building, training, and deploying AI models. Development and deployment tools help to manage the AI model lifecycle, including data prepara on, model training, tes ng, deployment, and monitoring. Finally, a user interface is devised, such as a graphical user interface (GUI), natural language interface, or API (Applica on Programming Interface) which allows humans to interact with and u lize the AI system. Figure 3: AI systems process ﬂow Cogni ve AI and Genera ve AI are two approaches within the broader spectrum of ar ﬁcial intelligence. Cogni ve AI focuses on mimicking human thought processes, emphasizing tasks such as percep on, reasoning, learning, and problem-solving. Genera ve AI is oriented towards crea ng fresh content, o en leveraging new methods like deep learning techniques. It excels in tasks involving crea ve outputs, including genera ng images, music, text, and even en re virtual environments. Both these approaches of Ar ﬁcial Intelligence encompass several key components, each playing a dis nct role in enabling machines to exhibit intelligent behavior. These components may work alone or in tandem to create powerful AI systems capable of learning, perceiving, solving problems, reasoning, and understanding human language. Artiﬁcial Intelligence Strategy Framework\n\n---\n\n### pp. 15-16: Learning is a core feature of AI, enabling machine\n\nLearning is a core feature of AI, enabling machines to acquire knowledge and improve their performance over me. Machine learning, the engine of AI, empowers systems to evolve and enhance their performance through experience. Unlike tradi onal programming, where rules are explicitly deﬁned, machine learning algorithms learn from data, adap ng and improving over me. This ability to learn is categorized into several approaches: A) Supervised Learning: In supervised learning, models are trained with labeled data, learning to map inputs to desired outputs. This method excels in tasks like image classiﬁca on, spam detec on, and predic ng customer churn. In simple terms - it is like teaching a child to iden fy fruits by showing them pictures and labels B) Unsupervised Learning system would si through unlabeled data, iden fying pa erns, grouping similar items, and uncovering hidden structures. This is invaluable for anomaly detec on, customer segmenta on, and recommenda on systems. In simple terms, it is like piecing together a puzzle without knowing the ﬁnal image. C) Reinforcement Learning: This process mimics how humans learn from rewards and consequences. The Reinforcement learning agents interact with an environment, taking ac ons and learning from the resul ng feedback in mul ple itera ons. This method is ideal for dynamic environments like robo cs, game playing, and self-driving cars. Deep learning employs ar ﬁcial neural networks2 comprising mul ple layers to uncover complex pa erns within data. It draws inspira on from the structure of the human brain. Deep learning models excel in intricate tasks including computer vision, facilita ng tasks such as object detec on, facial recogni on, and medical diagnosis. Addi onally, in natural language processing, they adeptly comprehend and generate human language, contribu ng to the development of chatbots, machine transla on systems, and sen ment analysis tools. Furthermore, in speech recogni on, deep learning algorithms convert spoken language into text, powering voice assistants, accessibility tools, and realme transcrip on services. Figure 4 : Ar ﬁcial Intelligence, Machine Learning and Deep Learning According to ISACA’s glossary, Neural networks are deﬁned as ‘Networks, inspired by the structure of the human brain, that learn by processing data through three layers (input, hidden, and output). They can be trained to match any input to various outputs, including binary ones, making them versa le tools in deep \n\n1. **Leveraging AI for Audit Data Analysis**:\n   - Ingesting millions of voucher records from IFMS/PFMS to identify round-tripping, duplicate vendor bank accounts, or split sanctions.\n2. **Critical Precautions When Interfacing with Public LLMs**:\n   - **Strict Prohibition**: Never expose sensitive, confidential, personal (PII), or classified government data into public commercial LLM interfaces (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini Public).\n   - **Data Leakage Risk**: Public LLMs may incorporate user prompts into global model training sets, violating official secrecy and the DPDP Act 2023.\n   - **Prompt Sanitization**: All data used with AI must be fully anonymized, de-identified, or processed on secure on-premise/government private cloud instances.\n3. **Principles of Responsible AI in Governance**:\n   - *Fairness & Non-Bias*: Ensuring algorithms do not discriminate based on demographic attributes.\n   - *Transparency & Explainability (XAI)*: Audit findings derived from AI must have an explainable audit trail; 'black-box' assertions cannot stand as formal audit evidence.\n   - *Accountability & Human-in-the-Loop*: Final audit judgment must always remain with human audit officers.\n4. **Risks in Government AI Systems**:\n   - Algorithmic bias in beneficiary selection (e.g. welfare denial), hallucination of facts, lack of legal accountability.",
