            "title": "2.1 DBMS and RDBMS Basics: Designing databases and principle of normalization",
            "content": "### DBMS Concepts and Principles of Database Normalization\n\n1. **DBMS Architecture**:\n   - Three-Schema Architecture: Physical level (internal storage), Conceptual level (logical table structures and relationships), External level (user views).\n   - *Data Independence*: Logical and physical data independence.\n2. **Data Redundancy and Anomalies**:\n   - Unnormalized tables suffer from Insertion Anomaly, Deletion Anomaly, and Update Anomaly.\n3. **Normalization Stages**:\n   - **First Normal Form (1NF)**: Eliminate repeating groups; ensure all attribute values are atomic (single-valued); each row uniquely identifiable.\n   - **Second Normal Form (2NF)**: Must be in 1NF AND eliminate partial functional dependencies (every non-key attribute must depend fully on the primary key, not part of a composite key).\n   - **Third Normal Form (3NF)**: Must be in 2NF AND eliminate transitive dependencies (no non-key attribute should depend on another non-key attribute: $A \\rightarrow B$ and $B \\rightarrow C$).\n   - **Boyce-Codd Normal Form (BCNF)**: For every functional dependency $X \\rightarrow Y$, $X$ must be a super key.",
            "gist": "DBMS three-schema architecture ensures data independence. Normalization removes update anomalies: 1NF (atomic values), 2NF (no partial dependency), 3NF (no transitive dependency).",
            "crux": "1. 1NF mandates atomic attribute values.\n2. 2NF removes partial dependency on composite keys.\n3. 3NF removes transitive dependency ($X \\rightarrow Y \\rightarrow Z$).\n4. Normalization minimizes redundancy and data corruption.",
