            "title": "3.3 Data Privacy: Principles of protecting personally identifiable information (PII)",
            "content": "### Protecting Personally Identifiable Information (PII)\n\n1. **Definition of PII**: Any information that can be used to distinguish or trace an individual's identity (Name, Aadhaar number, PAN, biometric records, bank account details, date of birth, mobile number).\n2. **Privacy Principles**:\n   - *Notice & Choice*: Clear disclosure of what data is collected and its intended use.\n   - *Purpose Limitation*: Data collected for a specific government scheme must not be repurposed without fresh consent.\n   - *Data Minimization*: Collect only what is strictly necessary for service delivery.\n   - *Storage Limitation*: Data must not be retained beyond the period necessary for fulfilling the purpose.\n3. **Audit Verification**: Ensuring pension databases, PF accounts, and welfare databases mask PII (masking first 8 digits of Aadhaar, encrypting bank account numbers).",
            "gist": "PII encompasses identifiers like Aadhaar, PAN, and biometrics. Privacy principles mandate purpose limitation, data minimization, secure storage, and masking.",
            "crux": "1. Data minimization: collect only necessary data.\n2. Masking Aadhaar (first 8 digits) is legally mandatory in public reports.\n3. Purpose limitation restricts repurposing citizen data.",
