            "title": "3.4 Information Asset Protection: Ensuring confidentiality, integrity, availability, encryption",
            "content": "### Information Asset Protection: The CIA Triad & Cryptographic Controls\n\n1. **The CIA Triad**:\n   - **Confidentiality**: Ensuring information is accessible only to authorized personnel (Access control lists, encryption at rest and in transit, multi-factor authentication).\n   - **Integrity**: Safeguarding the accuracy and completeness of data and processing methods (Cryptographic hashes - SHA-256, checksums, digital signatures, audit logs).\n   - **Availability**: Ensuring authorized users have timely, reliable access to information assets (Redundant power, RAID arrays, disaster recovery sites, DDoS protection).\n2. **Encryption Standards**:\n   - *Symmetric Encryption*: Single key for encryption and decryption (AES-256; fast, used for bulk data at rest).\n   - *Asymmetric Encryption*: Key pair - Public key encrypts, Private key decrypts (RSA-2048, ECC; used for digital signatures and TLS handshakes).\n   - *Hashing*: One-way mathematical function producing fixed-length digest (SHA-256; cannot be reversed; verifies file integrity).",
            "gist": "Information security rests on Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability (CIA). Symmetric AES-256 encrypts data; asymmetric RSA enables digital signatures; SHA-256 ensures integrity.",
            "crux": "1. Confidentiality: encryption and access control.\n2. Integrity: digital signatures and hash checks.\n3. Availability: redundancy and disaster recovery sites.\n4. AES-256 is the standard symmetric algorithm for data at rest.",
