            "id": "pc3_s3_c6",
            "chapterNum": 6,
            "title": "3.6 Common Cyber-Attack Methods: Understanding OWASP vulnerabilities and prevalent attack vectors",
            "content": "### OWASP Top 10 Web Application Vulnerabilities & Attack Vectors\n\n1. **A01: Broken Access Control**: Users can act outside their intended permissions (e.g. DDO viewing bills of another treasury).\n2. **A02: Cryptographic Failures**: Transmitting sensitive passwords/data in plain text (HTTP instead of HTTPS) or using weak algorithms (MD5, DES).\n3. **A03: Injection (SQL Injection - SQLi)**: Untrusted user input is concatenated directly into database queries, allowing attackers to bypass authentication or dump database tables.\n4. **A04: Insecure Design**: Architecture flaws and lack of threat profiling during software design.\n5. **A05: Security Misconfiguration**: Default passwords left active, verbose error messages revealing stack traces, unnecessary open ports.\n6. **A07: Identification and Authentication Failures**: Brute force attacks permitted without rate limiting, session fixation, weak passwords.\n7. **A08: Software and Data Integrity Failures**: Insecure software update pipelines, unverified plugins/libraries.",
            "gist": "OWASP Top 10 identifies critical web flaws: Broken Access Control, Cryptographic Failures, SQL Injection, Security Misconfigurations, and Authentication Failures.",
            "crux": "1. SQL Injection occurs when user input is not sanitized or parameterized.\n2. Broken Access Control allows horizontal/vertical privilege escalation.\n3. Cryptographic failures expose passwords and sensitive citizen data.",
