            "title": "3.9 Digital Personal Data Protection Act, 2023",
            "content": "### Digital Personal Data Protection Act (DPDP Act) 2023: Key Provisions\n\n1. **Core Terminology**:\n   - *Data Principal*: The individual to whom the personal data relates (the citizen/employee).\n   - *Data Fiduciary*: The entity (Ministry, Department, AG Office, Company) that determines the purpose and means of processing personal data.\n   - *Data Processor*: Entity processing data on behalf of Data Fiduciary (e.g. cloud provider, IT contractor).\n2. **Grounds for Processing**: Valid consent or certain legitimate uses (e.g. government welfare benefits, emergency medical, compliance with court orders).\n3. **Rights of Data Principals**: Right to access information, right to correction and erasure, right of grievance redressal, right to nominate.\n4. **Significant Data Fiduciary (SDF)**: Entities processing high volumes of sensitive data must appoint Data Protection Officer (DPO) and conduct Data Protection Impact Assessments (DPIA).\n5. **Penalties**: Severe financial penalties up to **Rs 250 Crore** for failure to prevent data breaches.",
            "gist": "DPDP Act 2023 governs digital personal data processing based on consent and legitimate uses. Establishes Data Principal rights, Data Fiduciary obligations, and penalties up to Rs 250 Crore.",
            "crux": "1. Data Principal = Individual citizen; Data Fiduciary = Government entity.\n2. Consent must be free, specific, informed, and unconditional.\n3. Maximum penalty: Rs 250 Crore for failure to take reasonable security safeguards.",
