            "title": "4.2 Control Objectives and Measures: General vs IS-specific controls, IT Audit vs IT-Assisted Audit",
            "content": "### IT Controls Taxonomy: General Controls vs Application Controls\n\n1. **General IT Controls (GITC)**:\n   - Apply across all software systems, IT infrastructure, and data centers.\n   - *Domains*: IT Governance, Logical Access Security (passwords, MFA), Change Management (testing, approval, deployment), Physical & Environmental Security, Business Continuity & Disaster Recovery (BCP/DR).\n2. **Application Controls (IS-Specific)**:\n   - Built into specific business applications (e.g. IFMS, PFMS, GeM):\n     - *Input Controls*: Field validation, range checks, format checks, mandatory fields (prevents negative values or future voucher dates).\n     - *Processing Controls*: Boundary checks, duplicate checks, transaction authorization limits.\n     - *Output Controls*: Reconciliation of totals, distribution controls, error exception logs.\n3. **IT Audit vs IT-Assisted Audit (CAATs)**:\n   - *IT Audit*: Auditing the IT system itself (evaluating security, architecture, source code, data integrity, and compliance of the software).\n   - *IT-Assisted Audit*: Using automated IT tools (SQL, Tableau, IDEA) to audit transactions and financial data of an entity.",
