            "content": "### Practical MS Excel Techniques for SAS Examination\n\n1. **Lookup Functions**:\n   - `VLOOKUP(lookup_value, table_array, col_index_num, [range_lookup])`:\n     - Limitations: Can only look left-to-right; breaks if columns are inserted.\n   - `INDEX & MATCH` (Industry Standard for Audit):\n     - `=INDEX(return_range, MATCH(lookup_value, lookup_range, 0))`\n     - Superior because it looks in any direction (left or right) and does not break with column changes.\n   - `XLOOKUP(lookup_val, lookup_arr, return_arr, [if_not_found], [match_mode])`: Modern replacement.\n2. **Pivot Tables & Power Pivot**:\n   - Summarizing millions of treasury transactions by DDO, Major Head, and Month.\n   - Creating calculated fields (e.g. Percentage Variance = `(Actual - Budget) / Budget`).\n   - Slicers and Timelines for interactive audit filtering.\n3. **Data Validation**:\n   - Restricting voucher input to valid dates, positive amounts, or predefined major heads from a dropdown list.\n4. **Conditional Formatting**:\n   - Highlighting duplicate voucher numbers, payments > Rs 25 Lakh, or negative balances using custom formula `=COUNTIF($A$2:$A$1000, A2)>1`.\n5. **What-If Analysis**:\n   - Goal Seek: Finding the required revenue collection to meet state fiscal deficit targets under FRBM.",
