            "title": "(iv) Introduction to accrual accounting in India – limitation of cash-based and advantages of accrual",
            "content": "### Accrual Accounting in Government: Limitations of Cash Basis & Reform Roadmap\n\n1. **Limitations of Cash-Based Government Accounts**:\n   - Does not record accrued liabilities (pending contractor bills, committed pension liabilities, unpaid interest).\n   - Omits government capital assets and infrastructure valuation; capital expenditure is written off in the year of payment.\n   - Distorts fiscal deficit through timing manipulation (delaying payments to April or advancing tax collections to March).\n2. **Advantages of Accrual Accounting**:\n   - Presents true and fair financial position showing total assets and liabilities on a Balance Sheet.\n   - Full cost accounting for government services and programs.\n   - Improved management of physical infrastructure and public debt.\n3. **Indian Roadmap**: 12th Finance Commission recommended progressive shift to accrual; GASAB has formulated pilot IGFRS standards for government entities.",
            "gist": "Cash accounting ignores unrecorded liabilities and capital asset values. Accrual accounting captures full liabilities, depreciates assets, and provides a complete government Balance Sheet.",
            "crux": "1. Cash basis allows artificial manipulation of fiscal deficits.\n2. Accrual captures total committed liabilities (pensions, contractor arrears).\n3. GASAB is piloting IGFRS accrual standards for Indian Government.",
