            "title": "a. Government budget – meaning, objectives and components",
            "content": "### Government Budgeting: Concepts, Objectives and Components\n\n1. **Concept**: A comprehensive legal and financial plan setting forth estimated receipts and proposed expenditures of the Government for a financial year (1st April to 31st March).\n2. **Core Fiscal Objectives**:\n   - *Allocation of Resources*: Reallocating public funds between economic sectors and social priorities.\n   - *Distribution of Income & Wealth*: Progressive taxation and welfare subsidies to reduce inequality.\n   - *Macroeconomic Stabilization*: Managing inflation, public debt, and business cycles.\n3. **Components of the Budget**:\n   - **Revenue Budget**: Revenue Receipts (Tax revenue + Non-tax revenue) and Revenue Expenditure (operational expenses, salaries, interest, subsidies).\n   - **Capital Budget**: Capital Receipts (recoveries of loans, borrowings, disinvestment) and Capital Expenditure (creation of physical assets, capital outlays, loans disbursed).",
            "gist": "A government budget balances Revenue Budget (taxes, operational expenses) and Capital Budget (borrowings, physical assets) to achieve allocation, distribution, and stabilization.",
            "crux": "1. Revenue expenditure does not create physical assets.\n2. Capital expenditure results in creation of permanent physical/financial assets.\n3. Budget period is 1st April to 31st March.",
